  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "An empty iron locomotive vanished into a 1912 Belgian tunnel. Seconds later, track bells began ringing miles down the line, alerting stationmasters to a phantom cargo barreling through the dark.",
        "visual_query": "old belgium railway tunnel",
        "visual_fallback": "vintage train tracks"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The massive iron engine breached a locked siding switch, defying every safety interlock designed to stop unauthorized movement. No driver sat at the copper throttle, yet the boiler pressure kept climbing into the danger zone.",
        "visual_query": "vintage steam train interior",
        "visual_fallback": "rusty train engine"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Railway dispatch logs showed zero telegraph signals sent to the crew. How does a multi-ton machine pilot itself past four active stations without hitting a single red signal?",
        "visual_query": "antique telegraph machine",
        "visual_fallback": "old railway office"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Investigators eventually discovered the regulator valve had been jammed open by a warped brass lever heated to extreme temperatures. When the crew had abandoned the overheating cab moments earlier, the expanding metal effectively locked the throttle wide open.",
        "visual_query": "brass steam valve",
        "visual_fallback": "vintage mechanical parts"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The runaway engine was stopped only when it hit a dead-end maintenance spur and derailed in a cloud of white steam. Could a simple piece of warped metal really turn a dead train into a driverless ghost?",
        "visual_query": "train derailment wreckage",
        "visual_fallback": "abandoned train tracks"
      }
    ],
